  "image_name": "0d409d1101949233b20ad4b64232f88e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways mobile genomic islands hijack or reprogram the conjugation machinery of helper elements to spread between bacteria. Across all four cartoons, a helper integrative element is shown integrated in the chromosome, then excised and used to drive transfer of a smaller island or genomic island through a type IV secretion system (T4SS).\n\nThe main message is that different islands exploit different parts of the helper’s transfer apparatus. One strategy is oriT mimicry, where the island carries a compatible origin of transfer and mobilization functions so it can be recognized by the helper relaxosome. Other strategies alter the relaxosome itself or modify mating-pair formation proteins, apparently replacing or redirecting components of the transfer machinery. In the SGI1-like example, the island contributes multiple functions, including transfer-related proteins and factors that seem to remodel the helper T4SS, enabling efficient mobilization. Another island appears to cooperate mainly through a relaxase/T4CP module while relying on the helper secretion system.\n\nOverall, the diagrams compare related but distinct parasitic mechanisms: excision from att sites, activation by helper-encoded regulators, capture of the helper conjugation system, and export of the island DNA through the membrane channel.",
